>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Contract-seam plate (portfolio ADR-004, ADR-014). Page-scoped: both
       sides report GREEN against their own copy of the shape, and the wire
       between them is broken. Not a second system diagram (the interactive
       map below already is that).

       The plate now hangs on the MONOGRAPH figure ladder. Its wrapper is
       <div class="fig fig--plate">, never a <figure>: a bare plate carries no
       two-slot caption, and a <figure> without data-fig fails the figure gate.
       So style.css owns the geometry (`.fig--plate > svg`, capped at
       --fig-plate) and this block owns only the drawing. The old max-width of
       260px is DELETED with that move, so the 1:1 discipline no longer holds
       and the plate owes a measurement: at a 320px viewport the spine track
       measures 272px over a 260-unit viewBox, which renders at scale 1.046.
       Every text class below therefore renders at or above its declared size,
       and the 9px microtext floor (CLAUDE.md) is met with margin. */
    .seam-plate .sp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* No text node carries this class today. It is held at the 9px floor so a
       later editor who reaches for it starts above the line, not under it. */
    .seam-plate .sp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-edge-break {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    /* GREEN: ink solid — the report each side believed. Word + stroke. */
    .seam-plate .sp-ok {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-ok-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    /* BROKEN: oxide — the seam. Word + oxide, not colour-only. */
    .seam-plate .sp-bad {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .seam-plate .sp-bad-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .seam-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .seam-plate marker path.sp-head-bad { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    /* The caption grammar itself now lives in style.css (ADR-014, OC-F
       closed), so the copy this page carried from context-architecture is
       DELETED with its origin comment. The plate's caption takes .fig-what and
       reads the consolidated note voice from there.

       The geometry that stayed behind is deleted too. A <div> still cannot
       hold a <figcaption>, so the consolidated `.fig figcaption` rule still
       cannot match a bare plate's paragraph — but style.css now matches that
       paragraph directly, through `.fig--plate > p`, and this island has
       nothing left to declare. */

    /* ts1-sequence, the seam drawn as a sequence (ADR-004, ADR-013).
       Page-scoped. A MAP, not a second verdict plate: .seam-plate above
       draws GREEN / GREEN / BROKEN, and this draws the sequence that
       produced it. Neither figure restates the other.

       max-width 260 keeps one viewBox unit at one CSS pixel down to 320px.
       The container is .wrap, whose gutter is 24px each side, so a 320px
       phone gives 272px. This plate sits inside a .decision card, and
       .decision carries `padding: 22px 0 30px`. The card adds no horizontal
       padding, so the plate measures the same 272px and renders at 260.

       This figure takes NO data-place attribute, and that is the A5 rule of
       ADR-014: a figure nested in a .decision keeps the entry's own measure
       and is never promoted. The width mechanism selects direct children of
       main.wrap, so an attribute here would state a width policy that nothing
       applies. The wrapper's own inline padding drops at 640px and below
       (PL-6, the pocket edition), which is what holds the 272px track above.

       The plate scale is therefore 1.000 at every width, so a declared unit is
       a rendered pixel and the 9px microtext floor is a code review rather
       than a measurement (CLAUDE.md, "Why 320px matters").

       The prefix is ts1- and the marker id carries the digit. A marker id is
       global to the document, this page already ships `sp-arrow` and `arrow`,
       and a duplicate id repaints the wrong arrow head where no gate sees it.

       NO OXIDE HERE, and that is deliberate. --accent-warm on this site marks
       a decision that was taken back. .seam-plate uses it for the broken
       wire, and this card is not a reversal, so a second oxide mark would
       state a reversal the page never claims. The untouched stub is muted and
       dashed instead: the file exists, and nobody updated it. The .ca-box-dead
       rule on context-architecture records the same split.

       Every state is a word plus a shape, because colour alone carries none:
       no gate reads a plate, and Geist has no glyph for a cross or a tick.
       SVG text inherits from <body>, so every text class names its own font.
       No digit (ADR-013): the counts stay in the prose above. */
    .ts1-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 0;
    }
    .ts1-plate .ts1-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    /* The stub exists and nobody touched it. Dashed, not absent. */
    .ts1-plate .ts1-box-dead {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
    }
    .ts1-plate .ts1-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .ts1-plate .ts1-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 8px broke the microtext floor. The plate renders at scale 1.000, so
       these four labels painted 8.00px at a 320px viewport and the floor is
       9px. The units go up and the plate keeps its geometry: the widest label
       grows from 144.2 to about 162 units inside a 260-unit box, and the
       tightest one ends about 5 units short of its box edge. */
    .ts1-plate .ts1-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .ts1-plate .ts1-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* The absent edge. Two stubs with a real space between them, because an
       absent connection is the claim and an absent line cannot be seen. */
    .ts1-plate .ts1-edge-dead {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
    }
    .ts1-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

